Iris recognition - simple implementation

Status
The seven Python files follow GroupProject.pdf. Dataset discovery, pipeline
wiring, iris localization, normalization, image enhancement, feature extraction,
iris matching, and performance evaluation are implemented.
The main script runs the session-1/session-2 experiment, prints the recognition
and threshold tables, and displays the dimension comparison and verification
ROC plots. run_experiment() also returns the results as a dictionary.

Run from this directory
    .venv/bin/python IrisRecognition.py
The full run extracts features and fits Fisher reduction for six dimensions,
so it takes longer than the small visual verification scripts below.

Visual verification scripts are in verify/:
    .venv/bin/python verify/localization_verify.py
    .venv/bin/python verify/normalization_verify.py
    .venv/bin/python verify/enhancement_verify.py
    .venv/bin/python verify/feature_extraction_verify.py
    .venv/bin/python verify/matching_verify.py
They show detected circles, normalized strips, before/after enhancement, and
the feature means/deviations for each filter channel. Normalization, enhancement,
and feature extraction also print output shapes and datatypes. Each script finds
the dataset and project modules relative to its own location.
The matching script uses three eyes, trains on session 1, and tests on session 2.
It prints true/predicted labels and subset accuracy for each measure, and plots
the distance matrices. These subset results are not the full 108-eye evaluation.
Set n_components=None in that script to inspect matching without Fisher reduction.

Packages
    numpy           -> import numpy as np
    opencv-python   -> import cv2
    scikit-learn    -> sklearn.discriminant_analysis and sklearn.metrics
    matplotlib      -> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
These imports are placed in the corresponding modules.
They are already installed in the existing .venv. To install in this environment:
    uv pip install --python .venv/bin/python numpy opencv-python scikit-learn matplotlib
For an environment with pip, use python -m pip install followed by those names.
SciPy is installed as a scikit-learn dependency; no direct SciPy usage is planned.

What we write and what packages handle
The assignment specifies the algorithm and asks us to explain its logic and
parameters. It does not explicitly require standard operations to be implemented
from scratch or give a package whitelist. The split below is our implementation
recommendation, not an instructor-stated rule. "Write" includes using NumPy
array operations; it does not mean writing every pixel operation as a Python loop.

- IrisRecognition: write the session split and stage orchestration (already
  present); pathlib finds files and cv2.imread reads the grayscale images.
- IrisLocalization: NumPy handles projections and center distances; OpenCV
  handles Otsu thresholding, connected components, smoothing, and Hough circles.
  The pupil uses its mask centroid and equal-area radius. For the outer iris,
  choose the Hough circle whose center is closest to the pupil.
- IrisNormalization: NumPy builds the source-coordinate grids from both
  circles using equation 2; cv2.remap performs bilinear interpolation. The
  output is float32 with the original intensity scale, normally 0..255.
- ImageEnhancement: NumPy handles the 16x16 block means and subtraction.
  cv2.resize uses INTER_CUBIC to expand the illumination estimate. cv2.normalize
  maps the corrected image's minimum/maximum to 0/255 as uint8, followed by
  cv2.equalizeHist on each 32x32 block. This uses ordinary local equalization.
- FeatureExtraction: NumPy constructs the paper's kernels and calculates 8x8
  block statistics; cv2.filter2D applies each kernel in floating point with
  reflected borders. Features are mean magnitude and mean absolute deviation,
  ordered by channel, then block row, then block column.
- IrisMatching: one iris_matching(train_features, train_labels, test_features,
  n_components=107) call fits LinearDiscriminantAnalysis(solver="svd") on
  training data, projects with features @ W using its learned scalings_, and
  calculates class centers and all three distances with NumPy. Its SVD
  transform() additionally centers data, which affects cosine distance, so
  projection is explicit. Classification uses nearest centers.
- PerformanceEvaluation: NumPy handles predictions, CRR, and genuine/impostor
  claim labels. roc_curve uses negative distances, then FMR=fpr and FNMR=1-tpr.
  Negate its thresholds to recover distance thresholds. Matplotlib plots the
  curves; reported rates are multiplied by 100 to give percentages.

API references
    https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d53/tutorial_py_houghcircles.html
    https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/da/d54/group__imgproc__transform.html
    https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d1b/tutorial_histogram_equalization.html
    https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/d86/group__imgproc__filter.html
    https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.discriminant_analysis.LinearDiscriminantAnalysis.html
    https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_curve.html

Dataset layout
    CASIA Iris Image Database (version 1.0)/001/1/*.bmp
    CASIA Iris Image Database (version 1.0)/001/2/*.bmp
    ... through eye 108
Use all three session-1 images per eye for training (324 images) and all four
session-2 images for testing (432 images). Keep this split fixed.

Design and implementation order
1. IrisLocalization.py: grayscale image -> pupil and iris (x, y, radius).
   Uses section 3.2.1's coarse pupil estimate and two threshold refinements.
   A simple OpenCV Hough search estimates the outer boundary with radii from
   80-130 px. If no circle is found, use a rough 105-pixel circle centered on
   the pupil. This fallback is an estimate, not a detected boundary. The code
   has five numbered steps with parameter explanations. It is intentionally
   naive: eyelids and weak iris boundaries can cause incorrect circles, and
   the full-pipeline recognition results are reported below.
2. IrisNormalization.py: image and circles -> 64x512 unwrapped image.
   Implements equation 2 in five numbered steps, with radial rows and
   counterclockwise angular columns. angle_deg changes the starting angle.
   Uses Y/rows for radial sampling and replicates border pixels when a circle
   extends beyond the image. This simple version does not mask eyelids.
3. ImageEnhancement.py: subtract illumination estimated from 16x16 block
   means and bicubic interpolation, then equalize each 32x32 region. Five
   numbered steps explain the implementation. Output is 64x512 uint8; global
   min-max scaling before equalization is our float-to-uint8 conversion choice.
   Independent block equalization can leave visible seams between blocks.
4. FeatureExtraction.py: filter the first 48 rows using the two circularly
   modulated kernels. Each 8x8 block contributes a mean magnitude and mean
   absolute deviation, for 1536 float32 features total (equations 3-6). Seven
   numbered steps explain the implementation. The starting frequencies are
   1/8 and 1/16 cycles/pixel. Kernels extend ceil(3 * max(sigma_x, sigma_y))
   pixels from the center, giving default sizes of 19x19 and 29x29.
5. IrisMatching.py: fit Fisher reduction on session 1, calculate one mean
   template per eye per angle, and match by L1, L2, and cosine distance.
   Take the minimum across angles -9, -6, -3, 0, 3, 6, 9 degrees.
   The single function has seven numbered steps and returns (classes, distances).
   distances is a dictionary keyed by "L1", "L2", and "cosine"; each matrix
   has shape (test images, classes), normally (432, 108). Predictions are
   classes[distances[measure].argmin(axis=1)]. Set n_components=None to use
   original features; for a subset of eyes, choose at most classes - 1 Fisher
   dimensions. Cosine distance assumes nonzero features and class templates.
6. PerformanceEvaluation.py: one function with seven numbered steps prints
   Table 3 (original/reduced CRR for L1, L2, and cosine), plots Figure 10 (cosine
   CRR versus Fisher dimensions), prints Table 4, and plots the verification
   ROC (FMR versus FNMR). Default dimensions are 20, 40, 60, 80, 100, and 107;
   the last dimension is used for Table 3's reduced column and verification.
   For a small subset, supply dimensions no larger than the number of eyes - 1.
   Verification uses cosine distance and every test-to-class claim. Accept a
   claim when distance <= threshold. Table 4 selects the largest thresholds
   with measured FMR <= 0.1%, 1%, and 5%, and reports the actual FMR and FNMR.
   These thresholds describe operating points on the test ROC. All returned
   rates are percentages. The result dictionary contains crr, dimensions,
   cosine_crr, threshold_table, roc, and figures; plots are displayed, not saved.
7. IrisRecognition.py: main() calls run_experiment(), which loads the dataset,
   extracts training/testing features, and calls performance_evaluation().

Each function documents its inputs, outputs, key parameters, and loops.

Paper details to preserve
- Ma et al. (2003), sections 3.2-3.4 and 4.3, are the implementation references.
- The two Gaussian (sigma_x, sigma_y) pairs are (3, 1.5) and (4.5, 1.5).
  Numeric carrier frequencies and finite kernel sizes are not specified in
  the paper. The frequencies and kernel sizes listed above are our simple
  implementation choices; they have not been tuned for recognition accuracy.
- Fisher reduction has at most classes - 1 directions: 107 for CASIA-IrisV1.
  The paper's 200-dimensional setting used a larger database.
- Equation 8 prints squared Euclidean distance for L2. Use it consistently;
  cosine matching uses 1 - cosine similarity so smaller scores are better.
- Table 3 compares original and reduced features. Figure 10 plots CRR against
  dimension. Table 4 reports thresholds, FMR, and FNMR. Figures 11/13 show ROC
  with FMR on the x-axis and FNMR on the y-axis. The assignment's reference
  numbers vary between slides; there is no Table 10 in the supplied paper.
- Image quality selection, bootstrap, and confidence intervals are outside
  the assigned implementation scope.

Measured results (November 3, 2026)
The full run used 108 eyes, 324 session-1 training images, and 432 session-2
test images with the default parameters. CRR percentages were:

    Measure     Original (1536-D)     Reduced (107-D)
    L1               87.50                89.35
    L2               82.41                89.58
    cosine           83.10                89.81

All three measures exceeded the assignment's >=80% identification target.
Cosine CRR at 20, 40, 60, 80, 100, and 107 Fisher dimensions was respectively
75.00%, 84.95%, 88.66%, 89.81%, 89.58%, and 89.81%.

Verification compared 432 genuine claims and 46,224 impostor claims using
107-D cosine distances. The following values are rounded for display:

    FMR limit (%)     Threshold     Actual FMR (%)     FNMR (%)
         0.1          0.002879           0.100          30.556
         1.0          0.003427           0.999          20.602
         5.0          0.004026           5.000          14.583

Limitations and improvement discussion to complete
Verification still rejects about 30.6% of genuine claims at the 0.1% FMR
operating point. The simple localization and lack of an occlusion mask remain
possible sources of error. Inspect incorrect matches to evaluate the effects
of boundary localization, eyelid/eyelash occlusion, and pupil deformation,
and explain improvements supported by further experiments.

